===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[remove]] a [[mask]] from someone.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[expose]], or [[reveal]] the [[true]] [[character]] of someone.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Hamlet Q1-2|act=I|scene=ii|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=jrU8AAAAYAAJ&pg=PT16|lines=35–36|passage=The Charieſt maide is prodigall enough, / If ſhe '''vnmaske''' hir beautie to the Moone.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To remove one's mask.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1749|author=Henry Fielding|title=The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling|passage=But instead of a direct answer to so important a question, Jones began to be very importunate with the lady to '''unmask'''; and at length having prevailed, there appeared not Mrs Fitzpatrick, but the Lady Bellaston herself.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To cease engaging in {{l|en|mask|masking|id=autismverb}}, to cease disguising one's [[autism]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Sarah Cobbe|title=Simple Autism Strategies for Home and School: Practical Tips, Resources and Poetry|publisher=Jessica Kingsley Publishers|isbn=9781784508173|page=49
|passage={{...}} to '''unmask''' and to assume her &#39;natural&#39; role (rather than her &#39;fitting in&#39; role).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Barry Carpenter; Francesca Happé; Jo Egerton|title=Girls and Autism: Educational, Family and Personal Perspectives|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781351234412|page=172
|passage=Autistic females are trying to adhere to social expectations placed upon them, and masking and blending to fit those expectations, {{...}} How do you '''unmask''' when you have spent a [long time masking].}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|military|dated}} To expose something that was [[conceal]]ed or [[shield]]ed from an [[enemy]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1893|author=w:Theodore Ayrault Dodge|title=Hannibal: a History of the Art of War Among the Carthaginians and Romans {{...}}|page=156|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=MVMZAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA156|text=He recalled his horse. This, retiring, '''unmasked''' the heavy infantry, which Hannibal called in from either flank and sent with a vigorous élan in close column across the ford against the ill-arrayed barbarians, followed by the cavalry, which had formed again in its rear.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Stephen R. Wise|chapter=To Capture an Island: Amphibious Operations in the Department of the South, 1861–1863|editor=Theodore P. Savas|title=Charleston: Battles and Seacoast Operations|page=10|pageurl=https://www.google.co.uk/books/edition/A_Journal_of_the_American_Civil_War_V5_2/Ip1aEAAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA10&printsec=frontcover|text=In less than an hour the batteries were '''unmasked'''. The Confederate works were visible and at 5:08 a.m., Seymour ordered the batteries to commence firing.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|computing}} To [[enable]] (an [[interrupt]], etc.) by unsetting or setting the associated [[bit]].

